          <!-- Section: Best Time To Visit -->
          <section id="best-time-to-visit" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-5">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                What Time Is Best To Visit Cu Chi Tunnels?
              </h2>
            </div>

            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              Morning is generally the most practical time to visit, especially if you want cooler conditions and more time for the rest of your day. The site opens at 7:00 AM, giving early visitors an opportunity to explore before the hottest part of the day. Morning also works particularly well for travelers who want to combine Cu Chi with another Ho Chi Minh City activity later in the day.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              <strong>The First Hour Is Usually The Quietest:</strong> If avoiding crowds is important, arriving close to opening time can be worthwhile. Travel operators and visitor reports indicate that the site can become considerably busier later in the morning as coach groups arrive.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              A departure around 7:00–8:00 AM is a practical planning window for travelers who want to avoid the hottest part of the day and return to Ho Chi Minh City around midday or early afternoon. This is one reason private transportation can be useful: you are not tied to the departure schedule of a large group. See our guide: <a href="/how-to-visit-cu-chi-tunnels-without-the-crowds" class="text-amber-800 hover:underline font-semibold">How to Visit Cu Chi Tunnels Without the Crowds</a>.
            </p>
          </section>

          <!-- Section: How long do you need -->
          <section id="how-long-do-you-need" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-5">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                How Long Do You Need At Cu Chi Tunnels?
              </h2>
            </div>

            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              Most travelers should allow around <strong>2 to 3 hours on site</strong>, depending on the visitor route, guiding, tunnel crawl, and optional activities.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              Your actual visit can be shorter or longer depending on which site you visit, your guide, crowd levels, whether you enter the tunnel, how much time you spend photographing the site, whether you explore additional areas, and whether you participate in optional activities.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              Around <strong>5 to 6 hours is a useful planning range</strong> for a private half-day excursion door-to-door, but actual travel time varies with traffic and hotel location. Trying to fit Cu Chi into a very tight three-hour door-to-door schedule leaves little room for traffic or delays. For road logistics, see our guide on <a href="/how-far-are-the-cu-chi-tunnels-from-ho-chi-minh-city" class="text-amber-800 hover:underline font-semibold">How Far Are The Cu Chi Tunnels From Ho Chi Minh City?</a>.
            </p>
          </section>

          <!-- Section: Do you have to crawl -->
          <section id="do-you-have-to-crawl" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-5">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                Do You Have To Crawl Through The Tunnels?
              </h2>
            </div>

            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              <strong>No. The tunnel crawl is optional.</strong>
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              Most of the visitor experience takes place above ground, including the forest paths, trapdoors, defensive displays and historical structures. The tunnel crawl is only one part of the visit.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              This is particularly useful for travelers with claustrophobia, taller travelers, larger-bodied travelers, older visitors, families with young children, and anyone who simply dislikes confined spaces.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              Visitor tunnel sections are available in different lengths, with travelers commonly reporting options around 20, 40, 60 and up to 100 metres. The underground experience is optional, and visitors can choose to stop early rather than completing the longest section. You do not need to force yourself through the tunnel to have a worthwhile visit.
            </p>

