          <!-- Section: Are tunnels original -->
          <section id="are-tunnels-original" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-5">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                Are The Tunnels Original?
              </h2>
            </div>

            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              The Cu Chi tunnel system is historically genuine, but the sections presented to visitors today have been <strong>adapted for tourism</strong>. Visitor passages have been widened or modified compared with the dimensions of the wartime tunnel system.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              This distinction matters. The heritage is real, but the modern tourist experience is not identical to entering an untouched wartime tunnel. Descriptions such as “crawl through the original tunnels” can therefore be misleading if they imply that today's visitor passage is unchanged.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              The conserved passages are extremely narrow, with UNESCO documentation recording varying dimensions across the three tunnel levels at Ben Duoc and Ben Dinh, with some sections measuring only around 62 to 65 cm wide.
            </p>

